WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.243 --> 00:00:09.126 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Iran's provinces, part 1 of 2. First, I clear the chosen names for
a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09.786 --> 00:00:20.792 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We work through 16 of the Iran regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:21.453 --> 00:00:23.578 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Ardabil into view.

4
00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:39.725 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ardabil lies in the far north-west of Iran, just inland from the Caspian shore
and tucked under the Caucasus, with the city of Ardabil as its largest city.

5
00:00:40.389 --> 00:00:51.477 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name appears as Ardavil in the medieval geography Hudud al-Alam, and the
shrine ensemble at Ardabil was placed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in
2010.

6
00:00:52.140 --> 00:01:00.801 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the north-western corner, where the great shrine of Ardabil and the
hot spa waters of Sareyn share the same high valleys.

7
00:01:01.489 --> 00:01:04.682 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring East Azerbaijan into view.

8
00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:17.828 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
East Azerbaijan fills the north-west of Iran around Tabriz, its largest city,
on the high plain that steps down to the Caspian Sea in the north.

9
00:01:18.482 --> 00:01:32.774 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The name Azerbaijan comes from Atropates, the Persian governor who held this
land after Alexander’s conquest, and at Kandovan people still live in homes
carved into the cones of volcanic rock.

10
00:01:33.438 --> 00:01:42.053 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the province of Tabriz, the cone houses dug into volcanic rock and
the name that came from Alexander’s own governor.

11
00:01:42.718 --> 00:01:46.596 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring West Azerbaijan into view.

12
00:01:49.433 --> 00:02:03.806 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
West Azerbaijan is the western half of Iranian Azerbaijan, with Urmia as its
largest city beside the great salt lake of the same name, and Kurdistan along
its southern border.

13
00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:20.444 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Lake Urmia was once the largest lake in the Middle East and has shrunk
drastically since the 1990s, while the Sasanian sanctuary of Takht-e Soleyman,
with one of the great fire temples of Zoroastrianism, stands near its southern
shore.

14
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:28.821 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the shrinking salt lake that was once the biggest in the Middle East,
with the Zoroastrian fire temple on its southern rim.

15
00:02:29.501 --> 00:02:31.289 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Gilan into view.

16
00:02:36.354 --> 00:02:47.186 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Gilan runs along the Caspian shore in the north-west, a green strip of forest
and rice between the mountains and the sea, with Rasht as its largest city.

17
00:02:47.845 --> 00:03:02.265 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The province is named for the Gelae, a people who lived here in antiquity, and
tea was first planted here around 1900 by Kashef al-Saltaneh, who brought the
plants from India.

18
00:03:02.925 --> 00:03:10.936 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the green tea coast of the Caspian, where the houses of Masuleh are
stacked up the hillside so that one roof is the next yard.

19
00:03:11.617 --> 00:03:14.891 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring North Khorasan into view.

20
00:03:18.156 --> 00:03:31.124 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
North Khorasan sits in the north-east of Iran, with Bojnurd as its largest
city, between the Turkmen plain of Golestan in the west and the old heartland
of Khorasan to the south.

21
00:03:31.789 --> 00:03:45.988 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was created in 2004 from the historical Khorasan, whose name refers to the
rising sun or the east, and the Kurdish communities around Bojnurd and Shirvan
descend from tribes the Safavids moved here.

22
00:03:46.651 --> 00:03:56.287 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the new province in the old sunrise land, where the wrestlers of
Esfarayen fight in heavy jackets called chukha.

23
00:03:56.963 --> 00:03:58.925 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Golestan into view.

24
00:04:03.735 --> 00:04:18.700 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Golestan lies on the Caspian shore in the north of Iran, with its largest city
Gorgan set back from the water, between Mazandaran in the west and North
Khorasan in the east.

25
00:04:19.353 --> 00:04:33.215 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name means the rose garden and it was split from Mazandaran in 1997, while
the Turkmen of its northern plain still breed horses and weave carpets of
their own style.

26
00:04:33.868 --> 00:04:42.262 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the rose garden on the Caspian, with the brick wall of Gorgan running
for two hundred kilometres behind the Turkmen plain.

27
00:04:42.945 --> 00:04:45.929 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Zanjan into view.

28
00:04:49.947 --> 00:05:01.290 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Zanjan sits in the north-west of Iran, a small province between the Caspian
plain and the Zagros, touching seven neighbours, with the city of Zanjan in
the middle.

29
00:05:01.948 --> 00:05:15.079 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name is of uncertain origin, and the dome of Soltaniyeh, built for the
Mongol ruler Oljeitu around 1310, is one of the largest brick domes in the
world.

30
00:05:15.734 --> 00:05:24.128 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the province with seven neighbours, whose salt mine has kept the
bodies of miners in its walls for centuries.

31
00:05:24.811 --> 00:05:26.936 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Mazandaran into view.

32
00:05:32.125 --> 00:05:44.199 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Mazandaran runs along the Caspian shore south of Gilan, a narrow strip of
forest and rice between the sea and the Elburz mountains, with Sari as its
largest city.

33
00:05:44.861 --> 00:05:58.642 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was historically known as Tabaristan, the Veresk Bridge carries the
Trans-Iranian Railway high through its mountains, and parts of its ancient
Hyrcanian forest are on the UNESCO World Heritage List.

34
00:05:59.293 --> 00:06:06.665 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the narrow green rice coast under the Elburz, where the railway
crosses the Veresk bridge above the valley floor.

35
00:06:07.344 --> 00:06:09.341 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Qazvin into view.

36
00:06:13.778 --> 00:06:25.075 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Qazvin lies in the north-west of Iran, just south of the Caspian Sea and west
of the Elburz mountains, between Mazandaran and Markazi, with the city of
Qazvin in its middle.

37
00:06:25.737 --> 00:06:38.230 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It became a separate province in 1997, and in 1090 Hasan Sabbah took control
of Alamut Castle in present-day Qazvin and made it the base of the Nizari
Ismailis.

38
00:06:38.889 --> 00:06:48.955 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the province that was split off in 1997, with the mountain fortress
of Alamut where Hasan Sabbah built his stronghold in 1090.

39
00:06:49.624 --> 00:06:52.259 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Alborz into view.

40
00:06:57.378 --> 00:07:08.849 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Alborz is the small province immediately south of the Caspian, wrapped around
the Elburz mountains, with Karaj as its largest city right below Tehran.

41
00:07:09.503 --> 00:07:29.195 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It takes its name from the Alborz mountains and was separated from Tehran
Province in 2010, and the Shah Abbasi caravanserai at Karaj was built under
the Safavid shah Soleiman and later served as a military base and an
agricultural school.

42
00:07:29.855 --> 00:07:37.018 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the youngest province around Tehran, with the Safavid caravanserai at
Karaj that later became a farm school.

43
00:07:37.706 --> 00:07:39.540 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Semnan into view.

44
00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:55.121 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Semnan lies in north-central Iran, running from the mountains below the
Caspian down to the edge of the salt desert, with Shahrud as its largest city
in the north.

45
00:07:55.783 --> 00:08:07.300 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Tarikhaneh mosque at Damghan, of the eighth century, is one of the oldest
in Iran, and the country launches its rockets from a spaceport in the desert
here.

46
00:08:07.958 --> 00:08:16.700 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the long narrow province between the mountains and the salt desert,
with the oldest mosque in Iran and a spaceport in the sand.

47
00:08:17.371 --> 00:08:21.040 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Kurdistan into view.

48
00:08:24.701 --> 00:08:36.938 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kurdistan sits in the north-west of Iran in the Zagros foothills, with
Sanandaj as its largest city, between West Azerbaijan in the north and
Kermanshah to the south-west.

49
00:08:37.598 --> 00:08:49.498 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name simply means land of the Kurds, and the stepped villages and
agricultural terraces of Hawraman here form part of a UNESCO World Heritage
cultural landscape shared with Kermanshah.

50
00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:57.267 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the north-western province of the Kurds, where the terraced villages
of Hawraman step down the mountainsides.

51
00:08:57.936 --> 00:09:00.699 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Tehran into view.

52
00:09:04.434 --> 00:09:16.764 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Tehran province is the heart of the north of the country, south of the Elburz
mountains and the Caspian Sea, with the capital of the same name on the plain
below the peaks.

53
00:09:17.419 --> 00:09:30.422 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The capital grew from a settlement near the older city of Rey, and Golestan
Palace there became a Qajar royal residence whose nineteenth-century
decoration mixes Persian craft with European influence.

54
00:09:31.085 --> 00:09:38.330 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the mountain-backed capital, where the green north of Iran meets the
dry central plain.

55
00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:42.320 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Hamadan into view.

56
00:09:45.776 --> 00:09:57.885 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Hamadan sits in the west of Iran at the eastern foot of the Zagros, with the
city of Hamadan in its middle, between Markazi in the east and Kurdistan in
the west.

57
00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:10.640 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Hamadan was the Ecbatana of the Greeks, capital of the Medes, and at Ganjnameh
the inscriptions of Darius and Xerxes are carved into the rock in three
languages.

58
00:10:11.303 --> 00:10:18.768 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the old Median capital, where two Achaemenid kings wrote their names
on the cliff in three scripts.

59
00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:22.213 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Qom into view.

60
00:10:26.646 --> 00:10:37.397 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Qom lies just south of Tehran in the centre of the country, at the northern
edge of the central desert, with the holy city of Qom as its largest city.

61
00:10:38.053 --> 00:10:49.907 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The law approving its separation from Tehran Province was passed in 1996, and
the shrine of Fatima Masumeh there honours the sister of Imam Reza and has
shaped the city’s pilgrimage traditions.

62
00:10:50.558 --> 00:10:58.058 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the small province on the northern edge of the desert, whose city
fills with pilgrims at the shrine of the imam’s sister.

63
00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:01.493 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Markazi into view.

64
00:11:05.237 --> 00:11:15.686 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Markazi lies in the centre-west of Iran, the heartland between the Zagros and
the central desert, with Arak as its largest city, to the south-west of
Tehran.

65
00:11:16.337 --> 00:11:29.596 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name means central in Persian, and the village of Farahan here is
associated with carpets bearing the Sarouk design, which takes its name from
another village in the same province.

66
00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:37.632 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the province whose name simply means the middle, and the carpet
pattern that carries another village’s name.

67
00:11:43.140 --> 00:12:06.000 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
16 regions of Iran, one for each target of the map: Ardabil, East Azerbaijan,
West Azerbaijan, Gilan, North Khorasan, Golestan, Zanjan, Mazandaran, Qazvin,
Alborz, Semnan, Kurdistan, Tehran, Hamadan, Qom and Markazi.

68
00:12:06.657 --> 00:12:16.502 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the shape of Iran from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
